infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title One CTE, two plans: inlined vs materialized
  desc Postgres 12 flipped the default — the same WITH clause now folds in or fences off, and predicate pushdown is what's at stake
  compares
    - label Inlined (NOT MATERIALIZED)
      children
        - label Predicate pushdown works
          desc The outer WHERE reaches into the CTE and hits an index — scan a slice, not the table
        - label Folded into the outer query
          desc Planned as if you'd written a subquery; the planner optimizes across the boundary
        - label Default when referenced once
          desc PG12+ inlines a CTE that is referenced once, side-effect-free, and non-recursive
        - label EXPLAIN shows no CTE Scan
          desc The CTE's tables appear directly in the main plan tree
    - label Materialized (MATERIALIZED)
      children
        - label Predicate pushdown blocked
          desc The fence — a selective outer WHERE can't reach in, so you may scan the whole base table first
        - label Computed once, then stored
          desc The full result is built once into memory, then the outer query filters that
        - label Default when reused or recursive
          desc Materialized when referenced more than once, data-modifying, or recursive
        - label EXPLAIN shows a CTE Scan
          desc A CTE Scan node reads from a separate 'CTE <name>' subplan
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
